codeExamples.code:
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class HelloController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Get() => Ok("Hello!");
}

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
{
    private static readonly List<string> Products = new() { "Keyboard", "Mouse" };

    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult GetAll() => Ok(Products);

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Add(string name)
    {
        Products.Add(name);
        return Ok(Products);
    }
}

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id:int:min(1)}")]
    public IActionResult GetById(int id) => Ok($"Order {id}");
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
ASP.NET Core's endpoint routing builds a route table at startup from every `[Route]`/`[Http*]` template across all controllers. For each incoming request, it matches the URL path and HTTP method against that table, scoring candidates by specificity -- more literal segments and route constraints generally make a template more specific than a bare parameter.

When exactly one route template matches with the highest specificity, that action is selected. When two or more templates are equally specific and both match the same URL and verb (for example, `GetById(int id)` mapped to `{id}` and `GetByName(string name)` also mapped to `{id}` with no differentiating constraint), ASP.NET Core cannot deterministically pick a winner and throws an `AmbiguousMatchException` at request time -- not at startup, which makes this class of bug easy to miss until the conflicting route is actually hit.

```csharp
[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult GetById(int id) => Ok(id);

[HttpGet("{code}")]
public IActionResult GetByCode(string code) => Ok(code);
// A request to GET /api/orders/42 matches both templates equally,
// since {id} and {code} are structurally identical placeholders.
// This throws AmbiguousMatchException at request time.
```

The fix is to disambiguate with route constraints (`{id:int}` vs `{code:alpha}`), with distinct literal prefixes (`"by-code/{code}"`), or by consolidating into a single action that branches on the parsed value. Because the conflict only manifests when a request actually lands on the overlapping path, it is good practice to write integration tests that exercise every documented route, not just manually spot-check a few URLs during development.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Minimal APIs let you define an HTTP endpoint as a single expression attached directly to the `WebApplication` instance, without declaring a controller class. They were introduced to reduce ceremony for small services, microservices, and simple CRUD surfaces where a full MVC controller hierarchy is unnecessary overhead.

The core methods mirror HTTP verbs: `MapGet`, `MapPost`, `MapPut`, `MapDelete`, `MapPatch`. Each takes a route template string and a delegate (commonly a lambda) that handles the request. Parameters on that delegate are bound automatically using the same rules as controller actions: route parameters are matched by name, simple types default to query string binding, and complex types default to the request body.

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/products", () => new[] { "Keyboard", "Mouse" });

app.MapGet("/products/{id:int}", (int id) => Results.Ok($"Product {id}"));

app.MapPost("/products", (Product product) =>
{
    // product is bound from the JSON request body
    return Results.Created($"/products/{product.Id}", product);
});

app.Run();
```

Return values are translated into HTTP responses automatically: returning a plain object serializes it as a 200 OK JSON body, while returning an `IResult` produced by the static `Results` class (or the newer, strongly-typed `TypedResults`) gives explicit control over status code, headers, and body -- `Results.Ok(...)`, `Results.NotFound()`, `Results.Created(...)`, `Results.BadRequest(...)`, `Results.NoContent()`, and so on.

Dependency injection works the same way as in controllers: any service registered in the DI container can simply be added as a parameter to the handler delegate, and the framework resolves it per-request without you writing a constructor.

Minimal APIs and controller-based APIs can coexist in the same project -- there is no requirement to choose only one style, and teams often use minimal APIs for small, focused endpoints while keeping controllers for larger, more structured resource groups.

codeExamples.code:
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

var products = new List<string> { "Keyboard", "Mouse" };

app.MapGet("/products", () => Results.Ok(products));

app.MapGet("/products/{id:int}", (int id) =>
    id < products.Count ? Results.Ok(products[id]) : Results.NotFound());

app.MapPost("/products", (string name) =>
{
    products.Add(name);
    return Results.Created($"/products/{products.Count - 1}", name);
});

app.Run();

lessons.contentMarkdown:
As a minimal API grows past a handful of endpoints, two features keep the code organized: route groups and typed results.

`MapGroup` creates a prefixed group of endpoints that share a common route prefix and, importantly, can share cross-cutting concerns such as authorization requirements, API versioning, OpenAPI tags, or rate limiting policies applied once to the whole group instead of repeated on every `Map*` call.

```csharp
var products = app.MapGroup("/api/products")
    .WithTags("Products")
    .RequireAuthorization();

products.MapGet("/", () => Results.Ok(GetAllProducts()));
products.MapGet("/{id:int}", (int id) => Results.Ok(GetProduct(id)));
products.MapPost("/", (ProductDto dto) => Results.Created($"/api/products/{dto.Id}", dto));
```

Minimal API parameter binding follows explicit source attributes when present (`[FromRoute]`, `[FromQuery]`, `[FromBody]`, `[FromServices]`, `[FromHeader]`) and otherwise infers the source using rules similar to controllers: route-matched names bind from the route, simple types default to the query string, complex types default to the JSON body, and types registered in DI are bound via `[FromServices]` inference when they are recognized as services.

`TypedResults` (as opposed to the non-generic `Results` class) returns a concrete type implementing `IResult` that also carries compile-time type information, which two things benefit from: unit tests can assert on the concrete result type directly instead of casting, and OpenAPI/Swagger generation can describe the possible response types and status codes more accurately because the method signature itself documents them.

```csharp
app.MapGet("/products/{id:int}", Results<Ok<Product>, NotFound> (int id) =>
{
    var product = FindProduct(id);
    return product is not null ? TypedResults.Ok(product) : TypedResults.NotFound();
});
```

- Prefer route groups once you have more than two or three related endpoints, to avoid repeating prefixes and policies.
- Prefer `TypedResults` over `Results` in new code when you want accurate OpenAPI metadata and easier testing.
- Remember that minimal API handlers do not automatically get the `[ApiController]` model-validation shortcut -- if you want automatic 400 responses for invalid models, you either validate manually or use a library such as MiniValidation/FluentValidation integrated into the pipeline.

codeExamples.code:
var orders = app.MapGroup("/api/orders").WithTags("Orders").RequireAuthorization();

orders.MapGet("/{id:int}", Results<Ok<string>, NotFound> (int id) =>
    id == 1 ? TypedResults.Ok("Order 1") : TypedResults.NotFound());

interviewQuestions.answer:
Both styles infer a binding source when no explicit `[From*]` attribute is present, but the inference rules are not identical, which is a common source of subtle bugs when developers copy a method signature from a controller into a minimal API handler or vice versa.

In a controller decorated with `[ApiController]`, the framework infers: route-matched parameter names bind from the route; other simple types (string, int, Guid, bool, etc.) bind from the query string; complex types that do not match a route parameter bind from the request body by default; and a type recognized as a registered service can bind via `[FromServices]` inference in some cases.

In a minimal API handler, the rules are similar for route and query binding, but there is no `[ApiController]`-style automatic complex-type-from-body inference triggered by "is this a complex type" alone -- instead minimal APIs infer `[FromServices]` for any parameter type that is registered in the DI container, and infer `[FromBody]` for complex types that are *not* recognized as a service and have no matching route parameter. If a complex DTO type happens to also be registered in DI (unusual, but possible if someone registers it as a configuration object), the inference could resolve it from services instead of the body, silently changing behavior.

```csharp
// Controller: 'filter' binds from query string as individual properties
// are NOT auto-mapped; a complex type with no route match binds from body.
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Search(SearchFilter filter) => Ok(filter); // body-bound

// Minimal API: identical-looking signature, same inference outcome here,
// but if SearchFilter were ALSO registered via builder.Services.AddSingleton<SearchFilter>(),
// it would instead be resolved from DI, not the request body -- a behavior
// that does not exist in the controller version.
app.MapPost("/search", (SearchFilter filter) => Results.Ok(filter));
```

The safest practice when porting handler signatures between the two styles, or when a parameter type's origin is not obvious from its name, is to make the binding source explicit with `[FromBody]`, `[FromQuery]`, or `[FromServices]` rather than relying on inference in either style.

`IEndpointFilter` is a pipeline mechanism specific to endpoints (both minimal API route handlers and, since .NET 8, usable with controller actions too) that runs **after** routing has matched a specific endpoint and **during** the invocation of that endpoint's handler -- as opposed to traditional middleware, which runs for every request regardless of which endpoint (if any) eventually matches, and has no inherent knowledge of the handler's parameters or return type.

The key practical differences:

- **Access to invocation context.** An `IEndpointFilter`'s `InvokeAsync` receives an `EndpointFilterInvocationContext` that exposes the already-bound arguments for the specific handler (`context.GetArgument<T>(index)`), something ordinary middleware cannot do because middleware runs before model binding for that endpoint occurs.
- **Short-circuiting with handler-aware results.** A filter can inspect bound arguments, short-circuit by returning a result directly (skipping the handler), or call `await next(context)` and then inspect/transform the handler's return value before it is written to the response.
- **Scoped, per-endpoint registration.** Filters are attached with `.AddEndpointFilter<T>()` directly on a specific `Map*` call or `MapGroup`, so they apply only to the endpoints you choose, without needing path-matching logic the way middleware often requires.

```csharp
public class ValidationFilter<T> : IEndpointFilter
{
    public async ValueTask<object?> InvokeAsync(
        EndpointFilterInvocationContext context, EndpointFilterDelegate next)
    {
        var arg = context.Arguments.OfType<T>().FirstOrDefault();
        if (arg is null) return Results.BadRequest("Missing payload.");
        // In a real filter, run validation logic against arg here.
        return await next(context);
    }
}

app.MapPost("/products", (Product p) => Results.Created($"/products/{p.Id}", p))
   .AddEndpointFilter<ValidationFilter<Product>>();
```

Because filters operate after binding and understand the handler's arguments and result, they are a better fit than middleware for concerns like per-endpoint validation, response shaping, or logging handler-level details (e.g., which arguments were bound). Middleware remains the right tool for concerns that must apply uniformly across the whole pipeline regardless of routing outcome, such as exception handling, authentication, or response compression.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Since .NET 6, the default Web API project template collapses what used to be two files -- `Program.cs` and `Startup.cs` -- into a single `Program.cs` written with **top-level statements**: C# code at the top level of a file, without an explicit `Main` method or enclosing class, that the compiler wraps into an entry point automatically.

A typical generated project includes:

- **Program.cs** -- the application's single entry point. It builds a `WebApplicationBuilder`, registers services into its `Services` collection (dependency injection container), builds a `WebApplication`, configures the HTTP request pipeline with middleware, and calls `Run()`.
- **appsettings.json / appsettings.Development.json** -- hierarchical configuration (connection strings, logging levels, feature flags), loaded automatically and layered by environment.
- **Controllers/** (or feature folders for minimal APIs) -- the classes or endpoint-mapping code that handle requests.
- **Properties/launchSettings.json** -- local development launch profiles (ports, environment variables, whether Swagger/browser should auto-open).
- **\*.csproj** -- the project file declaring the target framework (e.g., `net8.0`), NuGet package references, and build settings.

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Service registration: anything the app needs resolved via DI.
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();
builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();

var app = builder.Build();

// Middleware pipeline: executed in the order written, for every request.
if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseSwagger();
    app.UseSwaggerUI();
}

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();
```

`WebApplicationBuilder` is the unification point: it already knows how to read configuration from `appsettings.json`, environment variables, and command-line arguments, wires up a default logging provider, and exposes `builder.Environment` for environment-aware setup -- all without you writing the plumbing that `Startup.cs` used to require explicitly through `ConfigureServices`/`Configure`. The net result is less boilerplate for small-to-medium APIs while still supporting everything larger APIs need, including splitting configuration into extension methods for readability as a project grows.

codeExamples.code:
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")));

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();

lessons.contentMarkdown:
`Program.cs` has two distinct halves, and keeping the distinction clear is one of the most important mental models for working with ASP.NET Core.

**1. Service registration** happens before `builder.Build()` is called. Everything added via `builder.Services.Add*` methods goes into the dependency injection container -- `AddControllers()`, `AddDbContext<T>()`, `AddAuthentication()`, `AddAuthorization()`, `AddCors()`, `AddSwaggerGen()`, or your own services via `AddScoped`, `AddTransient`, `AddSingleton`. This section describes *what the application needs*, not *what happens to each request*.

**2. Pipeline configuration** happens after `builder.Build()` returns a `WebApplication`. Everything added via `app.Use*` and `app.Map*` methods builds the chain of middleware that every HTTP request flows through, in the exact order it is written. This section describes *what happens to each request*, step by step.

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// ---- Service registration (DI container setup) ----
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddAuthentication().AddJwtBearer();
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();

var app = builder.Build();

// ---- Pipeline configuration (middleware order matters) ----
app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();
```

Order matters very differently in each half: service registration order rarely matters (with a few exceptions, like configuring options before the services that consume them), while pipeline order is critical -- `UseAuthorization()` before `MapControllers()` protects routes, but after would let requests reach the controller before being checked.

As a project grows, it is idiomatic to extract related registrations into extension methods (`builder.Services.AddApplicationServices()`, `builder.Services.AddPersistence(builder.Configuration)`) defined in their own static classes, keeping `Program.cs` itself short and readable while still being the single, explicit place where the whole application is assembled -- there is no hidden convention-based wiring to search for elsewhere.

codeExamples.code:
// ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs
public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddPersistence(this IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration config)
    {
        services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
            options.UseSqlServer(config.GetConnectionString("Default")));
        services.AddScoped<IOrderRepository, OrderRepository>();
        return services;
    }
}

// Program.cs
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddPersistence(builder.Configuration);
builder.Services.AddControllers();
var app = builder.Build();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();

interviewQuestions.answer:
The two halves of `Program.cs` have fundamentally different execution models, which explains the asymmetry in how much call order matters.

`builder.Services.Add*` calls populate a dependency injection container's **service descriptor list** before anything runs. The container does not resolve or execute these services at registration time -- it simply records "when something asks for `IOrderService`, construct an `OrderService`." Because resolution happens later, on demand, the order in which you *register* independent services generally does not affect behavior: registering `AddControllers()` before or after `AddDbContext<T>()` makes no observable difference, because neither consumes the other at registration time.

`app.Use*`/`Map*` calls, by contrast, build an actual **chain of request delegates** that executes, in order, for every incoming HTTP request. Each middleware wraps the next one; `UseAuthorization()` registered before `MapControllers()` means an unauthorized request never reaches controller code, while the reverse order would let it through. This is executable, sequential pipeline construction, not declarative registration -- so order is semantically load-bearing.

The common exception to "registration order does not matter": **options configuration** and **decorator-style or dependent registrations**. If you configure an options class with `builder.Services.Configure<JwtOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("Jwt"))` and a later registration needs to read `IOptions<JwtOptions>` to construct itself as a singleton eagerly, the configuration must be visible by the time that singleton is constructed. Similarly, calling `AddAuthentication().AddJwtBearer(options => { ... })` after already calling `AddAuthentication()` elsewhere with a different default scheme can silently override the default scheme, because the later call's configuration wins.

```csharp
// Order-independent: fine either way
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(cs));

// Order-sensitive: the second AddAuthentication call's default scheme wins
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer();
```

In short: treat service registration as "assembling a parts catalog" (mostly order-independent) and pipeline configuration as "writing a sequential program that runs per request" (strictly order-dependent), while staying alert to registrations that configure shared, named, or singleton state, where later calls can override earlier ones.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Model binding is the process ASP.NET Core uses to populate action method parameters from an incoming HTTP request. Rather than manually parsing `HttpContext.Request`, you declare parameters on your action, and the framework fills them in from the appropriate part of the request.

The three most common explicit binding attributes are:

- `[FromRoute]` -- binds from a route template segment, such as `{id}` in `api/orders/{id}`.
- `[FromQuery]` -- binds from the query string, such as `?page=2&pageSize=20`.
- `[FromBody]` -- binds from the request body, deserializing JSON (via `System.Text.Json` by default) into the parameter's type. Only **one** parameter per action may be bound from the body, because the request body can only be read once as a stream.

```csharp
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Get(
    [FromRoute] int id,
    [FromQuery] bool includeDetails)
{
    return Ok(new { id, includeDetails });
}

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create([FromBody] CreateOrderDto dto)
{
    return Ok(dto);
}
```

With `[ApiController]` applied, you can usually omit these attributes: the framework infers `[FromRoute]` for any parameter whose name matches a route template token, infers `[FromQuery]` for other simple types (string, int, bool, Guid, DateTime, enums), and infers `[FromBody]` for a complex type with no matching route token. Other useful binding attributes include `[FromHeader]` (binds from a named HTTP header) and `[FromForm]` (binds from form-encoded or multipart data, commonly used for file uploads via `IFormFile`).

Binding failures are not exceptions you catch -- if a required value cannot be bound or converted (for example, a non-numeric string supplied where an `int` route parameter is expected), the framework records a model state error. Combined with `[ApiController]`, this becomes an automatic 400 response before your action method body ever executes, which is why defensive null/type checks for "did the right type arrive" are largely unnecessary in well-annotated APIs.

codeExamples.code:
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id:int}")]
    public IActionResult Get([FromRoute] int id, [FromQuery] string? status)
    {
        return Ok(new { id, status });
    }
}

public record CreateOrderDto(string CustomerName, decimal Total);

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create([FromBody] CreateOrderDto dto) => Ok(dto);

public class CreateOrderDto
{
    [Required]
    [StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 2)]
    public string CustomerName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Range(0.01, 100000)]
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
}

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Create(CreateOrderDto dto) => Ok(dto);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
These three attributes tell the model binding system which part of the HTTP request a given action parameter should come from.

- `[FromRoute]` reads the value from a URL segment captured by the route template, e.g., `{id}` in `api/orders/{id}`.
- `[FromQuery]` reads the value from the query string, e.g., `?page=2`.
- `[FromBody]` deserializes the value from the raw request body, typically JSON. Because the request body is a single stream, only one parameter per action can use `[FromBody]`.

When you omit these attributes on a controller decorated with `[ApiController]`, the framework applies inference rules: a parameter whose name matches a route template token binds from the route; other simple types (string, int, bool, DateTime, Guid, enums) bind from the query string; and a complex type that does not match any route token binds from the request body.

```csharp
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Get(int id, string? sort)
{
    // id -> inferred [FromRoute] because it matches the {id:int} token
    // sort -> inferred [FromQuery] because it is a simple type with no route match
    return Ok(new { id, sort });
}

[HttpPut("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Update(int id, UpdateOrderDto dto)
{
    // dto -> inferred [FromBody] because it is a complex type with no route match
    return Ok(dto);
}
```

Being explicit with these attributes is still good practice in any ambiguous case -- for instance, if you deliberately want a complex type bound from the query string (`[FromQuery] SearchFilters filters`), inference alone would send it to the body instead, so the attribute is required to override the default.

When a controller is decorated with `[ApiController]`, ASP.NET Core wires up an action filter (conceptually, via `ApiBehaviorOptions.InvalidModelStateResponseFactory`) that runs automatically as part of the action invocation pipeline, before your action method body executes.

The sequence is:

1. **Model binding** runs first, populating action parameters from route, query, headers, and body, and recording any binding failures (e.g., a non-numeric value where an int was expected) into `ModelState`.
2. **Validation** runs next: for each bound model, the framework walks its data annotation attributes (`[Required]`, `[Range]`, etc.) and, if the model implements `IValidatableObject`, calls `Validate`. Any failures are also recorded into `ModelState`.
3. Immediately before the action method executes, the `[ApiController]`-enabled pipeline checks `ModelState.IsValid`. If it is false, it never calls your action method at all -- instead it invokes `ApiBehaviorOptions.InvalidModelStateResponseFactory`, whose default implementation returns a `400 Bad Request` with a `ValidationProblemDetails` body: a JSON object with a `title`, `status`, and an `errors` dictionary mapping each invalid field name to an array of its error messages.

```csharp
// Default response body shape for a failed POST:
// {
//   "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7231#section-6.5.1",
//   "title": "One or more validation errors occurred.",
//   "status": 400,
//   "errors": {
//     "CustomerName": ["The CustomerName field is required."],
//     "Total": ["The field Total must be between 0.01 and 100000."]
//   }
// }
```

To customize this, you reconfigure `ApiBehaviorOptions` in `Program.cs`, typically to wrap the error payload in your organization's standard envelope, add a trace/correlation id, or log validation failures centrally:

```csharp
builder.Services.Configure<ApiBehaviorOptions>(options =>
{
    options.InvalidModelStateResponseFactory = context =>
    {
        var errors = context.ModelState
            .Where(e => e.Value?.Errors.Count > 0)
            .ToDictionary(e => e.Key, e => e.Value!.Errors.Select(x => x.ErrorMessage));
        return new BadRequestObjectResult(new { success = false, errors });
    };
});
```

This factory is the single customization point for every controller in the application, so changing it once standardizes error shape across the whole API rather than requiring per-controller boilerplate.

The HTTP request body is exposed to ASP.NET Core as a single, forward-only `Stream`. The JSON body-model binder consumes that stream exactly once to deserialize it into the target type. If two parameters were both marked `[FromBody]`, the second binder would find the stream already read (and, depending on buffering configuration, either empty or throwing), so ASP.NET Core enforces at most one `[FromBody]` parameter per action and throws an `InvalidOperationException` at startup (during endpoint metadata construction) if you violate this.

The standard workaround is to **wrap every body-bound value into a single DTO**:

```csharp
public record CreateOrderRequest(string CustomerName, decimal Total, List<OrderLineDto> Lines);

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create([FromBody] CreateOrderRequest request) => Ok(request);
```

This is the right approach whenever all the data is logically JSON. It has essentially no downside beyond requiring a wrapper type, which is good practice anyway (see DTOs vs. domain models).

A different situation arises when an endpoint must accept **both structured data and binary file content** in the same request -- JSON and raw file bytes cannot coexist in a single `application/json` body. Here, the correct approach is `multipart/form-data` with `[FromForm]`:

```csharp
public class UploadProductImageRequest
{
    public int ProductId { get; set; }
    public string Caption { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public IFormFile Image { get; set; } = default!;
}

[HttpPost("upload")]
public IActionResult Upload([FromForm] UploadProductImageRequest request) => Ok(request.ProductId);
```

With `[ApiController]`, a parameter containing an `IFormFile`/`IFormFileCollection` triggers automatic `multipart/form-data` inference, so `[FromForm]` can often be omitted, though being explicit avoids ambiguity when the action also has other parameters.

Trade-offs: multipart form data is less convenient for deeply nested structured fields than plain JSON (it is flat key/value pairs, with limited support for nested objects via bracket notation that some clients handle inconsistently), file size limits need explicit configuration (`Kestrel`'s `MaxRequestBodySize`, `RequestFormLimits`), and client libraries that default to sending pure JSON must be adjusted to construct multipart payloads instead. For large files, a common alternative is to accept metadata as JSON in one request and stream the file bytes in a second request (or via a pre-signed upload URL to blob storage), avoiding the multipart binding path entirely for very large payloads.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Controller actions in ASP.NET Core typically return one of two things: `IActionResult`, an interface representing "something that knows how to write an HTTP response," or `ActionResult<T>`, a generic wrapper that lets an action declare its "normal" success payload type while still allowing it to return other result types for error cases.

`ControllerBase` provides a large set of helper methods that create the common `IActionResult` implementations, so you rarely construct them directly:

```csharp
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Get(int id)
{
    var order = _repository.Find(id);
    if (order is null) return NotFound();      // 404
    return Ok(order);                           // 200 with order serialized as JSON
}
```

`ActionResult<T>` is useful specifically because it provides two things an `IActionResult` alone cannot: an implicit conversion operator, so `return order;` (returning the actual `T` value) compiles directly to an `Ok(order)`-equivalent response, and accurate OpenAPI/Swagger documentation of the success payload's shape, because the return type itself states the model (`ActionResult<Order>`) rather than the generic `IActionResult`.

```csharp
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public ActionResult<Order> Get(int id)
{
    var order = _repository.Find(id);
    if (order is null) return NotFound();   // still works: implicit conversion from NotFoundResult
    return order;                            // implicit conversion from Order to Ok(order)
}
```

A good rule of thumb: use `ActionResult<T>` whenever an action has one well-defined success payload type and some number of alternate non-success results (404, 400, 409, etc.) -- which describes the overwhelming majority of REST endpoints -- and reserve plain `IActionResult` for actions whose success response shape genuinely varies, or that never return a body at all (e.g., always `NoContent()`).

codeExamples.code:
public record Order(int Id, string CustomerName);

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private static readonly Dictionary<int, Order> Orders = new()
    {
        [1] = new Order(1, "Priya")
    };

    [HttpGet("{id:int}")]
    public ActionResult<Order> Get(int id)
    {
        if (!Orders.TryGetValue(id, out var order)) return NotFound();
        return order;
    }
}

lessons.contentMarkdown:
`ControllerBase` exposes helper methods for the status codes a REST API uses most often. Knowing precisely what each one does -- not just its number -- is essential both for writing correct APIs and for passing interviews that probe this detail.

- `Ok(value)` -- 200, serializes `value` as the response body. `Ok()` with no argument also returns 200 with an empty body.
- `Created(uri, value)` / `CreatedAtAction(actionName, routeValues, value)` -- 201, includes a `Location` header pointing at the newly created resource, with `value` serialized as the body. `CreatedAtAction` generates that URL by referencing another action method by name, which keeps the URL in sync automatically if the route template changes.
- `NoContent()` -- 204, no response body at all; conventional for successful `PUT`/`DELETE` operations that have nothing useful to return.
- `BadRequest(value?)` -- 400, used for malformed or semantically invalid client input that is not a validation-attribute failure (those are handled automatically under `[ApiController]`).
- `Unauthorized()` -- 401, the caller has not successfully authenticated at all (no valid credentials presented).
- `Forbid()` -- 403 in typical configurations (specifically, it triggers the configured authentication scheme's "forbid" behavior), meaning the caller is authenticated but lacks permission for the specific operation.
- `NotFound()` -- 404, the requested resource does not exist.
- `Conflict(value?)` -- 409, the request conflicts with the current state of the resource (e.g., a duplicate unique key, a concurrency token mismatch).
- `UnprocessableEntity(value?)` -- 422, syntactically valid but semantically invalid data, distinct from 400 in stricter REST conventions, though many APIs use 400 for both.

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public ActionResult<Order> Create(CreateOrderDto dto)
{
    var order = _repository.Add(dto);
    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = order.Id }, order);
}

[HttpDelete("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Delete(int id)
{
    var removed = _repository.Remove(id);
    return removed ? NoContent() : NotFound();
}
```

A subtlety worth remembering: `Unauthorized()` and `Forbid()` are often confused. `Unauthorized` (401) means "prove who you are" -- the correct response to a missing or invalid token. `Forbid` (403) means "I know who you are, and the answer is no" -- the correct response when a valid, authenticated user lacks the required role or policy. Returning the wrong one of these two makes client-side retry/login logic behave incorrectly, since a client typically responds to 401 by re-authenticating and to 403 by not retrying at all.

codeExamples.code:
[HttpGet("{id:int}", Name = "GetOrderById")]
public ActionResult<Order> Get(int id) =>
    Orders.TryGetValue(id, out var o) ? Ok(o) : NotFound();

[HttpPost]
public ActionResult<Order> Create(CreateOrderDto dto)
{
    var order = new Order(Orders.Count + 1, dto.CustomerName);
    Orders[order.Id] = order;
    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = order.Id }, order);
}

[HttpDelete("{id:int}")]
public IActionResult Delete(int id)
{
    if (!Orders.Remove(id)) return NotFound();
    return NoContent();
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
`Unauthorized()` produces an HTTP `401 Unauthorized` response, which, despite its name, actually means "authentication is required or has failed" -- the caller has not proven who they are, either because no credentials were supplied or because the supplied credentials (an expired/invalid token, wrong password) were rejected. The standard client reaction to 401 is to attempt authentication again: redirect to a login page, refresh a token, or prompt for credentials.

`Forbid()` produces an HTTP `403 Forbidden` response (specifically, it invokes the configured authentication scheme's challenge/forbid handler, which for most API scenarios results in a plain 403), which means "I know exactly who you are, and you are not allowed to do this." The caller is authenticated, but authorization -- checking roles, claims, or policies -- determined they lack permission for this specific resource or operation. The standard client reaction to 403 is to not retry the same request with the same credentials, since re-authenticating will not change the outcome.

```csharp
[HttpGet("admin-report")]
[Authorize] // requires authentication at all
public IActionResult GetAdminReport()
{
    if (!User.IsInRole("Admin"))
        return Forbid(); // authenticated, but not permitted -> 403

    return Ok(GenerateReport());
}
// A request with no token at all never reaches this method body --
// the [Authorize] attribute's authorization middleware returns 401 first.
```

In practice, most of this distinction is handled automatically by `[Authorize]` and the authentication/authorization middleware: a request with no valid credentials is rejected with 401 before your action runs, and `Forbid()`/403 is something you return explicitly from inside an action (or via a policy handler) when a specific business rule -- beyond simple role membership already checked by `[Authorize(Roles = "...")]` -- determines the authenticated user still cannot proceed.

`Forbid()` does not directly hardcode a status code. Instead, it invokes `HttpContext.ForbidAsync()`, which delegates to the currently configured authentication handler's `ForbidAsync` implementation for the request's authentication scheme (or the scheme specified explicitly, e.g., `Forbid(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)`, if multiple schemes are registered). Each authentication handler is free to implement "forbid" however is appropriate for its mechanism, which is precisely why the same `Forbid()` call can behave differently across authentication types.

- **JWT bearer authentication** (`JwtBearerHandler`): its `ForbidAsync` implementation simply writes an HTTP `403 Forbidden` status code to the response, with no body and no redirect, since there is no browser-based "challenge" concept for a bearer token API -- the client already presented a token, it is simply insufficiently privileged.
- **Cookie authentication** (`CookieAuthenticationHandler`), commonly used for browser-based apps: its default `ForbidAsync` implementation redirects the browser to a configured `AccessDeniedPath`, producing an HTTP redirect (302) to a "you do not have access" page rather than a bare 403 status code, because cookie authentication assumes an interactive browser context where showing a page makes more sense than a raw status code.

```csharp
// Program.cs - two schemes registered, each with its own Forbid behavior
builder.Services.AddAuthentication()
    .AddJwtBearer("Bearer", options => { /* ... */ })
    .AddCookie("Cookies", options =>
    {
        options.AccessDeniedPath = "/access-denied";
    });

// In a controller action:
return Forbid("Bearer");   // -> 403, no body
return Forbid("Cookies");  // -> 302 redirect to /access-denied
```

This is a common source of confusion when a project mixes a JSON Web API consumed by JavaScript or mobile clients with a cookie-authenticated admin section in the same application: calling `Forbid()` without specifying the scheme uses whatever the default authentication scheme is configured to be, and if that happens to be the cookie scheme, API clients expecting a clean 403 JSON response instead receive a 302 redirect response pointing at an HTML page, which most JSON-consuming HTTP clients handle poorly (some auto-follow redirects and then fail trying to parse HTML as JSON).

The fix in mixed-scheme applications is to be explicit: configure `DefaultForbidScheme` per area of the application, or call `Forbid(schemeName)` explicitly in API controllers so the correct, scheme-appropriate behavior is guaranteed rather than inherited implicitly from whichever scheme happens to be the application-wide default.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A Data Transfer Object (DTO) is a plain class or record whose only job is to describe the shape of data crossing an API boundary -- what a client sends in a request, or what the API returns in a response. A domain model (or an EF Core entity) describes the application's internal business concepts and data persistence shape. Conflating the two -- returning EF Core entities directly from controller actions -- is a common beginner shortcut that causes real problems as an API matures.

Reasons to separate them:

- **Over-posting / mass-assignment protection.** If a controller action binds the request body directly into an EF Core entity, a client can supply fields they should never be able to set -- like `IsAdmin` or `AccountBalance` -- and model binding will happily populate them, because model binding has no concept of "this field is internal." A DTO that simply does not declare those properties makes it structurally impossible for a client to set them.
- **Decoupling API shape from storage shape.** Database columns, navigation properties, and EF Core concerns (like lazy-loading proxies or circular references between related entities) are details of persistence, not of the public contract. Serializing an entity graph with circular navigation properties (`Order.Customer.Orders.First().Customer...`) can throw serialization exceptions or produce enormous, unintended payloads.
- **Independent evolution.** Renaming an internal domain property, or changing a database column type, should not automatically break every API consumer. A DTO layer absorbs that change in one mapping location.
- **Tailored payloads.** A list endpoint often needs only a few summary fields per item, while a detail endpoint needs the full object -- two different DTOs let each endpoint return exactly what it needs, rather than either over-fetching or awkwardly reusing one "do everything" model.

```csharp
// Domain/EF Core entity - has persistence concerns, navigation properties
public class Order
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string CustomerName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
    public bool IsInternalTestOrder { get; set; } // should never be client-settable
    public Customer Customer { get; set; } = default!; // navigation property
}

// DTO - deliberately exposes only what the API contract needs
public record OrderDto(int Id, string CustomerName, decimal Total);
```

The rule of thumb: entities model "what the business and database need to represent," DTOs model "what this specific API endpoint needs to send or receive." They will frequently look similar for simple resources, but treating them as the same type by convenience eventually produces either a security gap or an awkward, over-exposed public contract.

codeExamples.code:
// Vulnerable: binds the request body directly into the entity
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create(Order order)
{
    _db.Orders.Add(order);
    _db.SaveChanges();
    return Ok(order);
}

public record CreateOrderDto(string CustomerName, decimal Total);

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create(CreateOrderDto dto)
{
    var order = new Order { CustomerName = dto.CustomerName, Total = dto.Total };
    _db.Orders.Add(order);
    _db.SaveChanges();
    return Ok(new OrderDto(order.Id, order.CustomerName, order.Total));
}

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Once you commit to separate DTO and domain/entity types, you need a mapping strategy to convert between them at the API boundary. There are three common approaches, in increasing order of automation.

**1. Manual mapping.** Write the conversion by hand, either as a constructor, a static factory method, or an extension method. This is the most explicit and easiest to debug, and is entirely adequate for small-to-medium APIs.

```csharp
public static class OrderMappingExtensions
{
    public static OrderDto ToDto(this Order order) =>
        new(order.Id, order.CustomerName, order.Total);

    public static Order ToEntity(this CreateOrderDto dto) =>
        new() { CustomerName = dto.CustomerName, Total = dto.Total };
}

[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public ActionResult<OrderDto> Get(int id)
{
    var order = _db.Orders.Find(id);
    return order is null ? NotFound() : order.ToDto();
}
```

**2. A mapping library**, such as AutoMapper or Mapster. These use configuration (often convention-based, matching properties by name) to generate the mapping logic, reducing boilerplate for larger object graphs at the cost of some "magic" -- mapping mistakes can be less visible at compile time, and debugging a misconfigured map sometimes requires understanding the library's own internals.

```csharp
// AutoMapper profile
public class OrderProfile : Profile
{
    public OrderProfile()
    {
        CreateMap<Order, OrderDto>();
        CreateMap<CreateOrderDto, Order>();
    }
}

// Usage in a controller (with IMapper injected)
var dto = _mapper.Map<OrderDto>(order);
```

**3. Source generators**, such as Mapperly, which generate mapping code at compile time based on attributes, giving AutoMapper-like convenience with zero runtime reflection cost and compile-time errors for mismatched properties.

For most teams, the deciding factor is object graph complexity and team size: manual mapping scales fine for straightforward, flat DTOs and keeps everything debuggable with "go to definition"; a mapping library earns its complexity once there are dozens of entities with nested, frequently-changing shapes, and the convenience of convention-based mapping outweighs the reduced explicitness.

codeExamples.code:
public record OrderDto(int Id, string CustomerName, decimal Total);

public static class OrderMapping
{
    public static OrderDto ToDto(this Order order) =>
        new(order.Id, order.CustomerName, order.Total);
}

[HttpGet]
public ActionResult<IEnumerable<OrderDto>> GetAll() =>
    Ok(_db.Orders.Select(o => o.ToDto()).ToList());

interviewQuestions.answer:
Binding a request body directly to an EF Core entity (or any domain model with more settable properties than the API contract intends to expose) introduces an **over-posting** (also called mass-assignment) vulnerability: because model binding populates every public, settable property it finds a matching JSON key for, a malicious or merely curious client can include extra fields in their request body that correspond to real properties on the entity but were never meant to be client-controlled -- things like `IsAdmin`, `AccountBalance`, `CreatedAt`, or `OwnerId`.

```csharp
public class User
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Email { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public bool IsAdmin { get; set; } // never meant to be client-settable
}

[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Register(User user) // binds directly to the entity
{
    _db.Users.Add(user);
    _db.SaveChanges();
    return Ok(user);
}
// POST { "email": "x@example.com", "isAdmin": true }
// -> creates a new admin user, because IsAdmin has a public setter
// and model binding has no concept of "this property is restricted."
```

The two main mitigations:

1. **Use a dedicated DTO for input that structurally omits sensitive fields.** A `RegisterUserDto` with only `Email` and `Password` properties makes it impossible for a client to set `IsAdmin` through that endpoint, because the type simply has no such property for the binder to populate.
2. **If binding directly to a domain type is unavoidable for some reason, explicitly allow-list bindable properties** using `[Bind(nameof(User.Email))]` (an older, more error-prone technique since it requires remembering to update the allow-list whenever the entity gains fields) -- in modern ASP.NET Core, the DTO approach is strongly preferred over this because it fails safe: a newly added entity property is excluded from binding by default, rather than included by default.

The DTO approach is the one virtually every current ASP.NET Core security guideline recommends, precisely because its safety does not depend on a developer remembering to update an allow-list every time the entity changes.

Two common technical problems, distinct from the over-posting security issue, arise specifically from EF Core's navigation properties and change-tracking machinery leaking into the serialization layer.

**1. Circular reference / self-referencing JSON serialization failures.** EF Core entities commonly have bidirectional navigation properties -- an `Order` has a `Customer`, and that `Customer` has a `List<Order>` back-reference, which itself contains the original `Order` again. `System.Text.Json`, by default, throws a `JsonException` ("a possible object cycle was detected") when it encounters this during serialization, because it has no inherent way to know the graph is meant to be read as a tree rather than fully expanded.

```csharp
public class Order { public Customer Customer { get; set; } = default!; }
public class Customer { public List<Order> Orders { get; set; } = new(); }

// return Ok(order); where order.Customer.Orders contains 'order' itself
// -> throws System.Text.Json.JsonException: A possible object cycle was detected.
```

Workarounds like `ReferenceHandler.Preserve` (which emits `$id`/`$ref` markers instead of throwing) technically fix the exception but produce a non-standard, awkward JSON shape that most API consumers cannot parse without special handling -- which is itself a sign the entity should not have been serialized directly in the first place.

**2. Lazy-loading proxy exceptions or unintended N+1 queries during serialization.** If lazy loading is enabled (via proxies), accessing a navigation property during JSON serialization -- which happens implicitly as the serializer walks every public property -- can trigger a database query per navigation property per entity in a collection, or throw an `ObjectDisposedException`/`InvalidOperationException` if the `DbContext` has already been disposed by the time serialization actually runs (which can happen depending on how action results are executed relative to the request scope).

```csharp
// If order.Customer is a lazy-loaded navigation property and the DbContext
// backing it has already been disposed when System.Text.Json later
// accesses order.Customer during response serialization:
// -> throws ObjectDisposedException: Cannot access a disposed context instance.
```

Both problems disappear entirely once you map to a DTO before returning: the DTO contains only scalar, intentionally-selected fields, so there are no navigation properties for the serializer to walk, no cycles to detect, and no lazy-loading triggers left to fire. This is one more reason the DTO boundary is considered a correctness fix, not only a security one.

Instead of loading complete entity graphs via `_db.Orders.Include(o => o.Customer).ToList()` and then mapping each entity to a DTO in application code, EF Core lets you project directly into the DTO shape inside the LINQ query itself, using `Select`. Because EF Core translates `Select` into SQL, the database is asked to return only the columns the DTO actually needs, rather than every column of every involved table.

```csharp
public record OrderSummaryDto(int Id, string CustomerName, decimal Total);

var summaries = await _db.Orders
    .Select(o => new OrderSummaryDto(o.Id, o.Customer.Name, o.Total))
    .ToListAsync();
// Generates SQL selecting only Id, Customer.Name (via a join), and Total --
// not every column of Order or Customer, and no entity materialization,
// change tracking, or lazy-loading proxies are involved at all.
```

Benefits over "load entities, then map":

- **Less data transferred from the database.** Only the columns the projection references appear in the generated SQL `SELECT` list, which matters a great deal for wide tables or large text/blob columns the DTO does not need.
- **No change tracking overhead.** Because the result is a DTO, not a tracked entity, EF Core does not need to set up change-tracking snapshots for it, which is faster and uses less memory for read-only / list endpoints.
- **No lazy-loading or circular reference risk.** The navigation property access (`o.Customer.Name`) is translated into a SQL join at the database level; nothing is lazy-loaded at the .NET object level, and no entity graph with back-references is ever materialized.
- **Avoids N+1 queries implicitly.** Because the projection is one LINQ expression, EF Core generates one SQL statement (typically with joins) for the whole result set, rather than the application code looping and triggering a lazy load per item.

The limitations: projection expressions must be translatable to SQL, so you cannot call arbitrary C# methods, custom extension methods, or complex conditional logic with side effects inside the `Select` -- only what the EF Core LINQ provider recognizes. You also lose the ability to later call `_db.SaveChanges()` against a projected DTO, since it is not a tracked entity, so write operations still need the entity-loading path. And it requires maintaining the projection expression as the DTO shape evolves, which some teams offload to a mapping library's "ProjectTo" feature (AutoMapper's `ProjectTo<T>()`, for instance, builds the `Select` expression from the same mapping configuration used for in-memory mapping, keeping both in sync).

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Every HTTP request handled by ASP.NET Core flows through a pipeline built from **middleware**: small components, each wrapping the next, that can inspect or modify the request, decide whether to pass control further down the pipeline, and inspect or modify the response on the way back out. Conceptually, each middleware is a function that receives an `HttpContext` and a `RequestDelegate` representing "the rest of the pipeline," and either calls that delegate (continuing the chain) or does not (short-circuiting it).

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Before: {context.Request.Path}");
    await next(context);   // calls the next middleware in the chain
    Console.WriteLine($"After: {context.Response.StatusCode}");
});
```

The pipeline is built once, at startup, from the sequence of `app.Use*`/`app.Map*` calls in `Program.cs`, and that exact sequence is what every request executes, in order, every time -- there is no per-request reordering. This is why pipeline order is one of the first things to check when debugging unexpected behavior: a middleware registered too late never gets a chance to affect requests that an earlier middleware already short-circuited (for example, returned a response directly without calling `next`).

Conceptually, the pipeline can be visualized as nested layers, like an onion: the request enters from the outside, passes through each middleware's "before next()" code going inward, reaches the innermost layer (ultimately, the matched endpoint), and then passes back outward through each middleware's "after next()" code on the way to the client. Middleware that needs to run logic on the way out (such as logging the final response status code, or modifying response headers after the endpoint has written its body in some scenarios) relies specifically on code placed after the `await next(context)` call.

A middleware that never calls `next(context)` **short-circuits** the pipeline -- no middleware after it, and no endpoint, ever executes for that request. This is exactly how something like a custom "maintenance mode" middleware, or `UseStatusCodePages`, intercepts a request before it reaches further processing.

codeExamples.code:
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    var start = DateTime.UtcNow;
    await next(context);
    var elapsed = DateTime.UtcNow - start;
    Console.WriteLine($"{context.Request.Path} took {elapsed.TotalMilliseconds}ms, status {context.Response.StatusCode}");
});

app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello");
app.Run();

app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    if (context.Request.Path == "/blocked")
    {
        context.Response.StatusCode = 403;
        await context.Response.WriteAsync("Access denied");
        return; // next() is never called
    }
    await next(context);
});

app.MapGet("/blocked", () => "You should never see this");

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddAuthentication().AddJwtBearer();
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddPolicy("Default", p => p.WithOrigins("https://app.example.com").AllowAnyHeader().AllowAnyMethod()));

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseRouting();
app.UseCors("Default");
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();

app.Run();

interviewQuestions.answer:
`UseAuthentication()` is the middleware responsible for examining incoming credentials (a cookie, a bearer token, an API key -- whatever schemes are configured) and, if valid, populating `HttpContext.User` with a `ClaimsPrincipal` representing the authenticated identity and its claims. `UseAuthorization()` is the middleware that decides whether the current request is *allowed* to proceed, by checking `HttpContext.User` against the `[Authorize]` requirements (roles, policies, schemes) attached to the matched endpoint.

Authorization logically depends on authentication having already run, because there is nothing to authorize against otherwise -- `HttpContext.User` would either be null or an anonymous, unauthenticated principal. If you register them in the wrong order:

```csharp
app.UseRouting();
app.UseAuthorization();   // WRONG ORDER: runs before authentication populates User
app.UseAuthentication();
app.MapControllers();
```

every request is evaluated for authorization against an unauthenticated `HttpContext.User` (typically an empty, anonymous principal), because the authentication middleware that would have populated real claims has not run yet by the time authorization checks occur. The practical symptom is that every `[Authorize]`-protected endpoint incorrectly rejects even requests that supply perfectly valid credentials, returning 401/403 regardless of the token's validity, since from the authorization middleware's point of view, no one is logged in yet.

```csharp
// Correct order
app.UseRouting();
app.UseAuthentication(); // populates HttpContext.User first
app.UseAuthorization();  // then checks it against endpoint requirements
app.MapControllers();
```

ASP.NET Core does not throw a startup exception for this particular ordering mistake (unlike, say, calling `UseAuthorization()` before `UseRouting()`, which does throw), which makes it a subtler bug to track down -- the application runs and compiles fine, but every protected endpoint behaves as if no one is ever logged in.

Middleware is not simply "runs once and returns a value" -- the `async (context, next) => { ... }` pattern means a single middleware instance effectively straddles both the inbound and outbound legs of the request, because the code before `await next(context)` executes on the way in, and the code after it executes on the way out, after everything deeper in the pipeline (including the eventually matched endpoint) has already run and produced a response. This is true regardless of whether the endpoint was known at the time the "before" portion ran, since the "after" portion only resumes once the entire rest of the pipeline -- including routing and endpoint execution -- has completed.

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    // "before": endpoint is NOT yet known here, UseRouting() has not run
    await next(context);
    // "after": by this point, routing AND the endpoint have already executed;
    // context.GetEndpoint() now returns the matched endpoint, and
    // context.Response already has its final status code (if not yet sent)
    context.Response.Headers.Append("X-Served-By", "api-pod-3");
});

app.UseRouting();
app.MapControllers();
```

This is exactly how response-modifying middleware (compression, custom headers, response caching) can be registered early in the pipeline and still correctly observe/modify the final response -- it is not reading anything about the endpoint on the way *in*, only acting on the fully-formed response on the way *out*.

A concrete production bug this ordering subtlety causes: a custom "add a correlation ID response header" middleware registered **before** `UseExceptionHandler()` (or more precisely, after it, where it should be) can fail to add its header when an unhandled exception occurs downstream, if the exception-handling middleware re-executes the pipeline against a different request path (as `UseExceptionHandler("/error")` does internally, by re-invoking the pipeline against the configured error path) *after* the correlation-middleware's "before" code already ran once for the original, now-failed request, and the middleware is not written to handle running again correctly for the re-executed error path. The specific, commonly seen symptom: a response header that is reliably present on successful (200-range) responses but mysteriously absent specifically on 500 error responses, because the header-writing logic assumed it would always run exactly once per logical request, not once per physical pipeline execution (and `UseExceptionHandler`'s re-execution means it can effectively run the downstream pipeline more than once for what the client experiences as a single request).

```csharp
app.UseExceptionHandler("/error"); // on exception, internally re-executes pipeline against /error
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    context.Response.OnStarting(() =>
    {
        context.Response.Headers.Append("X-Correlation-Id", context.TraceIdentifier);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    });
    await next(context);
});
app.UseRouting();
app.MapControllers();
// A well-written version uses Response.OnStarting to defer the header write
// until just before the response actually begins, which correctly handles
// both the normal path and the re-executed /error path, instead of writing
// the header directly in the "after next()" code, which can run at the
// wrong point relative to UseExceptionHandler's internal re-execution.
```

The general lesson: middleware ordering is not only about "what runs before the endpoint is known" but also about correctly reasoning through re-execution semantics introduced by middleware like `UseExceptionHandler` that can invoke the downstream pipeline more than once per client-visible request.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
ASP.NET Core supports two equivalent ways to write custom middleware: inline, using `app.Use(...)` with a lambda, and as a dedicated class following the middleware convention.

**Inline lambda middleware** is the quickest way to add simple, pipeline-wide behavior directly in `Program.cs`:

```csharp
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    context.Response.Headers.Append("X-Powered-By", "CodeAtlas");
    await next(context);
});
```

This is fine for small, self-contained logic, but it becomes unwieldy for anything non-trivial, and it cannot be unit tested in isolation the way a class can.

**Class-based middleware** follows a simple convention: a public class with a constructor that accepts a `RequestDelegate next` (and optionally other singleton-scoped services), and a public `InvokeAsync` (or `Invoke`) method that accepts `HttpContext` (and, importantly, can accept additional parameters resolved via dependency injection per call -- more on that in the next lesson) and returns a `Task`.

```csharp
public class RequestTimingMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public RequestTimingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var sw = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
        await _next(context);
        sw.Stop();
        context.Response.Headers.Append("X-Response-Time-Ms", sw.ElapsedMilliseconds.ToString());
    }
}
```

You register a class-based middleware with `app.UseMiddleware<T>()`:

```csharp
app.UseMiddleware<RequestTimingMiddleware>();
```

A critical detail: ASP.NET Core constructs **one instance of the middleware class for the lifetime of the application** (not one per request), because the middleware pipeline itself is built once at startup. This means anything injected into the middleware's **constructor** is effectively treated as a singleton for the middleware's lifetime, which has direct consequences for dependency injection, covered in the next lesson -- constructor-injecting a scoped service (like a `DbContext`) into middleware is a common and dangerous mistake for exactly this reason.

codeExamples.code:
public class RequestTimingMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    public RequestTimingMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var sw = System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch.StartNew();
        await _next(context);
        sw.Stop();
        context.Response.Headers.Append("X-Response-Time-Ms", sw.ElapsedMilliseconds.ToString());
    }
}

// Program.cs
app.UseMiddleware<RequestTimingMiddleware>();
app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello");

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Because ASP.NET Core instantiates a class-based middleware exactly once, as a singleton, for the entire lifetime of the application pipeline, you must be careful about *where* you inject dependencies into it.

**Constructor injection** into middleware only works safely for services registered as **singleton** (or, with caveats, stateless transient services that do not themselves depend on scoped services). Injecting a **scoped** service -- most notably an EF Core `DbContext`, which is scoped to each HTTP request by default -- into a middleware's constructor is a classic bug: the scoped service would be captured once, at application startup, and reused across every subsequent request, which is incorrect and can throw `InvalidOperationException` ("Cannot resolve scoped service from root provider") or, worse, silently share state across unrelated requests.

The correct pattern for scoped dependencies is **method injection**: add the dependency as an additional parameter to `InvokeAsync` itself, rather than to the constructor. The runtime resolves method parameters from the **current request's** DI scope each time `InvokeAsync` is called, which is exactly the lifetime a scoped service needs.

```csharp
public class AuditLogMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    // Safe: RequestDelegate is effectively a fixed delegate, not a scoped service
    public AuditLogMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    // Safe: AppDbContext is resolved fresh from the CURRENT request's scope
    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context, AppDbContext db)
    {
        await _next(context);
        db.AuditLogs.Add(new AuditLog
        {
            Path = context.Request.Path,
            StatusCode = context.Response.StatusCode
        });
        await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

Middleware can also be configured via the **options pattern**, keeping configuration out of the middleware class itself:

```csharp
public class RateLimitOptions
{
    public int MaxRequestsPerMinute { get; set; } = 60;
}

builder.Services.Configure<RateLimitOptions>(builder.Configuration.GetSection("RateLimit"));

public class RateLimitMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly RateLimitOptions _options;

    public RateLimitMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, IOptions<RateLimitOptions> options)
    {
        _next = next;
        _options = options.Value; // IOptions<T> itself is a safe singleton to inject
    }

    public Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context) => _next(context);
}
```

`IOptions<T>` is itself registered as a singleton by the options system, so injecting it into a middleware constructor is always safe, even though the underlying configuration values it wraps might conceptually feel "per-request" -- the options object itself does not change per request unless you specifically use `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` (scoped) or `IOptionsMonitor<T>` (singleton, supports change notifications), each suited to different reload scenarios.

codeExamples.code:
public class AuditLogMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    public AuditLogMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context, AppDbContext db)
    {
        await _next(context);
        db.AuditLogs.Add(new AuditLog { Path = context.Request.Path, StatusCode = context.Response.StatusCode });
        await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(connectionString));
app.UseMiddleware<AuditLogMiddleware>();

interviewQuestions.answer:
Inline middleware, registered with `app.Use(async (context, next) => { ... })` directly in `Program.cs`, defines the middleware's behavior as a single lambda expression living right where it is registered. Class-based middleware instead follows a convention: a public class with a constructor accepting a `RequestDelegate next` parameter, and a public `InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)` method (optionally with additional dependency-injected parameters) containing the actual logic, registered separately via `app.UseMiddleware<T>()`.

```csharp
// Inline: concise, lives entirely in Program.cs
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    context.Response.Headers.Append("X-App-Version", "1.0");
    await next(context);
});

// Class-based: logic lives in its own file/class, independently testable
public class AppVersionMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    public AppVersionMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        context.Response.Headers.Append("X-App-Version", "1.0");
        await _next(context);
    }
}
// Program.cs
app.UseMiddleware<AppVersionMiddleware>();
```

Both produce functionally identical behavior in the pipeline -- the choice is primarily about code organization and testability, not capability. Inline lambdas are well suited to small, self-contained, one-off behavior that is easy to read in place and unlikely to need unit testing in isolation (a quick response header, a simple short-circuit for a health-check path). Class-based middleware is preferable once the logic grows non-trivial, needs its own dependencies beyond `RequestDelegate`, benefits from being unit-tested independently of the full pipeline, or is reused across multiple projects -- a dedicated class can be instantiated and tested directly, while an inline lambda embedded in `Program.cs` is awkward to isolate and test on its own.

A practical rule of thumb many teams follow: if a middleware's logic would take more than a few lines, or ever needs injected dependencies beyond the `next` delegate, reach for the class-based convention from the start, since migrating from an inline lambda to a class later is mechanical but easy to defer indefinitely once the inline version 'already works.'

ASP.NET Core builds the middleware pipeline exactly once, at application startup, by constructing a single instance of each class-based middleware. Because of this, any service injected via that middleware's **constructor** is resolved exactly once, from the application's root DI container, for the entire lifetime of the app -- not once per request.

A **scoped** service, such as an EF Core `DbContext` registered via `AddDbContext<T>()` (scoped by default), is designed to live for the duration of a single HTTP request and then be disposed. Resolving it at the constructor level of a singleton-lifetime middleware breaks that contract in one of two ways, depending on configuration:

1. If the DI container's scope validation is enabled (the default in the Development environment), attempting to resolve a scoped service from the root provider throws an `InvalidOperationException` at startup: *"Cannot resolve scoped service ... from root provider."*
2. If scope validation is somehow bypassed, the *same* `DbContext` instance would be captured once and reused across every subsequent request indefinitely -- since `DbContext` is not thread-safe and accumulates change-tracking state, this leads to concurrency exceptions, stale tracked entities being reused across unrelated requests, and data corruption under concurrent load.

```csharp
// WRONG: DbContext resolved once at startup, shared across every request
public class AuditLogMiddleware
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db; // captured once, never refreshed
    public AuditLogMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, AppDbContext db) { _db = db; }
    // ...
}

// CORRECT: DbContext resolved fresh, per request, via method injection
public class AuditLogMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    public AuditLogMiddleware(RequestDelegate next) => _next = next;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context, AppDbContext db)
    {
        await _next(context);
        // db here is a correctly-scoped instance for THIS request
    }
}
```

The fix is **method injection**: add the scoped dependency as a parameter to `InvokeAsync`/`Invoke` instead of the constructor. ASP.NET Core resolves method parameters from the current HTTP request's own DI scope each time the method is invoked, which matches the lifetime the scoped service actually needs. This is specific to middleware -- controllers do not have this problem because a new controller instance is constructed per request, so constructor injection of scoped services there is always correct.

All three interfaces expose configured option values, but they differ in lifetime and in whether they reflect configuration changes made after application startup (via a reloadable configuration source, such as `appsettings.json` with `reloadOnChange: true`).

- **`IOptions<T>`** is registered as a **singleton**. It computes the options object once, the first time it is resolved, and returns that same cached instance for the entire lifetime of the application -- it never reflects configuration changes made after that first resolution. Because it is a singleton, it is always safe to inject into anything else that is also singleton-lifetime, including a middleware constructor.
- **`IOptionsSnapshot<T>`** is registered as **scoped**. It recomputes the options object once per scope (effectively, once per HTTP request in a typical web app), picking up any configuration changes that occurred since the last snapshot. Because it is scoped, injecting it into a middleware constructor has exactly the same problem as injecting a `DbContext` into a middleware constructor: it would be captured once at startup from the root provider and never refreshed, defeating its entire purpose and potentially throwing the same root-provider scope-validation exception.
- **`IOptionsMonitor<T>`** is registered as a **singleton**, but unlike `IOptions<T>`, it actively supports change notifications: calling `.CurrentValue` always returns the latest configured values, and you can subscribe to `.OnChange(...)` to react immediately when configuration changes, without needing a new scope at all. This makes it the correct choice for genuinely "always current" configuration inside a singleton-lifetime component like middleware.

```csharp
public class FeatureFlagMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly IOptionsMonitor<FeatureFlags> _flags;

    public FeatureFlagMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, IOptionsMonitor<FeatureFlags> flags)
    {
        _next = next;
        _flags = flags; // singleton-safe, and always reflects the latest reloaded config
    }

    public Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        if (!_flags.CurrentValue.MaintenanceModeEnabled) return _next(context);

        context.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status503ServiceUnavailable;
        return context.Response.WriteAsync("Service is temporarily down for maintenance.");
    }
}
```

Using `IOptions<T>` in this scenario would mean a configuration change (e.g., an operator flipping `MaintenanceModeEnabled` to `true` via a config file reload) would never be observed by the already-constructed middleware instance, since `IOptions<T>` caches its value forever after first resolution -- exactly the kind of bug that only shows up when an operator tries to use a "hot-reloadable" feature flag during an incident and finds it has no effect until the application is restarted. `IOptionsMonitor<T>` is specifically designed to avoid that gap while remaining safe for singleton-lifetime components like middleware.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Rather than wrapping every controller action in try/catch blocks, ASP.NET Core provides `UseExceptionHandler` middleware to catch unhandled exceptions from anywhere later in the pipeline in one central place, and translate them into a consistent HTTP error response.

The classic approach re-executes the pipeline against a dedicated error-handling endpoint when an exception occurs:

```csharp
var app = builder.Build();

if (!app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseExceptionHandler("/error");
    app.UseHsts();
}
else
{
    app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage(); // detailed stack traces, Development only
}

app.MapGet("/error", (HttpContext context) =>
{
    var feature = context.Features.Get<IExceptionHandlerFeature>();
    var exception = feature?.Error;

    return Results.Problem(
        title: "An unexpected error occurred.",
        statusCode: StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError,
        detail: app.Environment.IsDevelopment() ? exception?.ToString() : null);
});

app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

Key points:

- `UseDeveloperExceptionPage()` is only ever appropriate in Development: it returns full exception details, including stack traces and source code snippets, directly in the HTTP response -- a serious information disclosure risk if ever enabled in production.
- `UseExceptionHandler("/error")` re-executes the request pipeline against the `/error` path when an exception is caught, preserving the original exception via `IExceptionHandlerFeature`/`IExceptionHandlerPathFeature`, so the error endpoint can log it and shape a safe response without exposing internals to the client.
- `Results.Problem(...)` (and its controller equivalent, `Problem(...)` on `ControllerBase`) produces an RFC 7807-compliant **Problem Details** response: a standard JSON shape (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`) that many HTTP clients and API gateways already understand how to parse consistently, rather than every API inventing its own ad-hoc error JSON shape.
- `builder.Services.AddProblemDetails()` registers a service that automatically applies the Problem Details format to error status codes returned throughout the application, including ones not explicitly produced by your own `Results.Problem` calls, for broader consistency.

Centralizing exception handling this way means individual controllers stay focused on their own logic, with no repeated try/catch boilerplate for generic "something unexpected went wrong" cases -- only genuinely action-specific error handling (like catching a specific domain exception to return a particular 409 Conflict) belongs inside an individual action.

codeExamples.code:
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
builder.Services.AddControllers();
var app = builder.Build();

app.UseExceptionHandler("/error");

app.MapGet("/error", (HttpContext context) =>
{
    var feature = context.Features.Get<IExceptionHandlerFeature>();
    return Results.Problem(title: "An unexpected error occurred.", statusCode: 500, detail: app.Environment.IsDevelopment() ? feature?.Error.ToString() : null);
});

app.MapGet("/boom", () => { throw new InvalidOperationException("Something broke"); });
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();

lessons.contentMarkdown:
.NET 8 introduced `IExceptionHandler`, a dedicated abstraction for writing exception-handling logic as a discrete, testable, dependency-injectable class, rather than as a lambda inside a re-executed `/error` endpoint.

```csharp
public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    private readonly ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> _logger;

    public GlobalExceptionHandler(ILogger<GlobalExceptionHandler> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext, Exception exception, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _logger.LogError(exception, "Unhandled exception for {Path}", httpContext.Request.Path);

        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Invalid request"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new
        {
            title,
            status = statusCode,
            traceId = httpContext.TraceIdentifier
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true; // true = this handler fully handled the exception
    }
}
```

Registration requires two steps: registering the handler(s) with the DI container, and registering the Problem Details service (used as a fallback/supporting formatter), then enabling the middleware with no path argument, since handling logic now lives in the handler class rather than a re-executed endpoint:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseExceptionHandler(); // no path needed; delegates to registered IExceptionHandler(s)
```

Multiple handlers can be registered, and they are tried **in registration order**: each handler's `TryHandleAsync` returns `true` if it fully handled the exception (stopping the chain) or `false` to let the next registered handler (or, if none remain, the default Problem Details response) attempt to handle it instead. This makes it straightforward to have a specific handler for a particular exception type (say, a domain validation exception) registered before a catch-all general handler, cleanly separating concerns that used to require a large switch statement inside a single `/error` endpoint.

Compared to the `UseExceptionHandler("/error")` endpoint-based approach, `IExceptionHandler` is unit-testable in isolation (it is just a class implementing an interface, with `TryHandleAsync` callable directly in a test), avoids the conceptual overhead of "re-executing the pipeline against a fake path," and composes more cleanly when multiple distinct categories of exceptions need different handling logic.

codeExamples.code:
public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(HttpContext httpContext, Exception exception, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var statusCode = exception is KeyNotFoundException ? 404 : 500;
        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new { status = statusCode, message = exception.Message }, ct);
        return true;
    }
}

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
var app = builder.Build();
app.UseExceptionHandler();
app.MapGet("/orders/{id:int}", (int id) => { if (id != 1) throw new KeyNotFoundException(); return Results.Ok("Order 1"); });

interviewQuestions.answer:
`UseDeveloperExceptionPage()` is middleware designed exclusively to help developers debug exceptions locally: when an unhandled exception occurs, it returns an HTML page directly in the HTTP response containing the full exception type, message, stack trace, the specific source file and line number (if PDB symbols are available), and even a view of relevant request details like headers, cookies, and query parameters.

In production, exposing any of this to an end user (or, worse, to an attacker probing the API) is a serious information disclosure risk: stack traces can reveal internal class and method names, library versions with known vulnerabilities, file system paths, and sometimes even fragments of connection strings or other configuration values embedded in exception messages -- all of which help an attacker map the application's internals far more effectively than a generic "500 Internal Server Error" response ever would.

```csharp
if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
{
    app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage(); // detailed errors, local debugging only
}
else
{
    app.UseExceptionHandler("/error"); // generic, safe error response
    app.UseHsts();
}
```

The default Web API project template guards against accidentally shipping this to production by branching on `app.Environment.IsDevelopment()`, which reads the `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT` environment variable at startup. As long as deployment pipelines correctly set `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production` (or any non-"Development" value) outside of local development, this check reliably keeps the detailed exception page out of production builds. The risk in practice comes from misconfigured deployments where that environment variable is left unset or incorrectly set to "Development" in a production or staging environment that is reachable by the public internet -- which is why many teams additionally add deployment-time checks or startup assertions to fail fast if `IsDevelopment()` is ever true outside of an explicitly local context.

When an unhandled exception propagates up through the pipeline to `UseExceptionHandler` middleware, it does not simply forward the exception to a separate, independent request. Instead, it performs an in-process **re-execution**: it resets what it safely can of the current `HttpContext` (clearing the response body buffer if it has not already been sent to the client, and resetting the request path to the configured error path, e.g., `/error`), stores the original exception and the original request path into request features (`IExceptionHandlerFeature.Error` and `IExceptionHandlerPathFeature.Path`), and then re-invokes the pipeline's routing and endpoint-execution machinery against this modified path -- all within the same `HttpContext` and the same underlying TCP connection/request.

```csharp
app.MapGet("/error", (HttpContext context) =>
{
    var feature = context.Features.Get<IExceptionHandlerPathFeature>();
    var originalPath = feature?.Path;       // the path that originally threw, e.g. "/orders/42"
    var exception = feature?.Error;          // the original exception instance
    return Results.Problem(title: exception?.Message, statusCode: 500);
});
```

Critical consequence: because this is a **re-execution of the same `HttpContext`**, anything that was already written to the response **before** the exception occurred cannot be undone -- if your action had already started streaming a response body (common with large JSON payloads using `IAsyncEnumerable` streaming, or manual `Response.Body` writes) before throwing partway through, `UseExceptionHandler` cannot cleanly replace a partially-sent response with a clean error response, because HTTP headers (including the status code) may have already been flushed to the client. In that specific case, the middleware detects that the response has already started and does **not** attempt to re-execute, instead simply logging the exception and letting the (now broken, partially-written) response terminate as-is, usually resulting in a client-side error like an unexpectedly truncated JSON body rather than a clean error status.

A concrete production bug from this: an endpoint that streams a large result set using `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and `Results.Ok(asyncEnumerable)` writes response headers (status 200) and begins streaming JSON array elements immediately, before the full result set -- and therefore any exception from, say, item 500 out of 1000 -- has been evaluated. If the underlying database connection drops mid-stream, the exception is thrown *after* the 200 status and partial JSON have already been sent to the client, so `UseExceptionHandler` cannot retroactively turn this into a 500 Problem Details response; the client instead receives a 200-status response with a truncated, invalid JSON body, which can be more confusing to diagnose than a clean error would have been, because the HTTP-level status code actively lies about what happened.

```csharp
// Streaming large results defers evaluation, which means an exception partway
// through can occur AFTER headers (status 200) are already sent --
// UseExceptionHandler can no longer intervene cleanly at that point.
app.MapGet("/export", async IAsyncEnumerable<OrderDto> () =>
{
    await foreach (var order in _repository.StreamAllOrdersAsync())
    {
        yield return order.ToDto(); // if this throws at item 500, status 200 was already sent
    }
});
```

The practical mitigation is to validate everything that can reasonably fail (connection availability, authorization, basic query validity) **before** writing any part of the response, and to treat genuinely late-breaking failures during a long-running stream as a case that needs its own handling (such as a sentinel error object in the stream, or client-side detection of a truncated response), rather than assuming the global exception handler is a safety net that applies uniformly regardless of how much of the response has already been sent.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
Entity Framework Core's `DbContext` is the central class representing a session with the database: it tracks entities, translates LINQ queries into SQL, and coordinates saving changes within a unit of work. In a Web API, you define a context class per bounded area of your data model, declare a `DbSet<T>` property per entity type you want to query, and register it with the dependency injection container.

```csharp
public class AppDbContext : DbContext
{
    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> options) : base(options) { }

    public DbSet<Order> Orders => Set<Order>();
    public DbSet<Customer> Customers => Set<Customer>();
}
```

Registration happens in `Program.cs` via `AddDbContext<T>()`, which configures the context's lifetime as **scoped** by default -- meaning one instance is created per HTTP request and disposed at the end of that request, which matches EF Core's change-tracking model correctly (you generally do not want to share a single context instance across concurrent requests, since it is not thread-safe).

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options =>
    options.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")));

var app = builder.Build();
```

Once registered, any controller can receive the context via ordinary constructor injection, since controllers are also instantiated per request, matching the context's scoped lifetime exactly:

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;

    public OrdersController(AppDbContext db)
    {
        _db = db;
    }

    [HttpGet]
    public async Task<ActionResult<IEnumerable<Order>>> GetAll() =>
        Ok(await _db.Orders.ToListAsync());
}
```

A few practical notes: always prefer the asynchronous EF Core methods (`ToListAsync`, `FindAsync`, `SaveChangesAsync`, etc.) inside a Web API, since blocking the request-handling thread on synchronous database I/O (`ToList()`, `SaveChanges()`) wastes thread-pool capacity under load and reduces the number of concurrent requests the server can handle. Connection strings should come from configuration (`appsettings.json`, environment variables, or a secret store), never hardcoded in source, since they typically contain credentials.

codeExamples.code:
public class AppDbContext : DbContext
{
    public AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> options) : base(options) { }
    public DbSet<Order> Orders => Set<Order>();
}

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(o => o.UseSqlServer(builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("Default")));

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public OrdersController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    [HttpGet]
    public async Task<ActionResult<IEnumerable<Order>>> GetAll() => Ok(await _db.Orders.ToListAsync());
}

lessons.contentMarkdown:
A standard resource controller backed by EF Core typically implements the four CRUD operations using a small, consistent set of `DbContext`/`DbSet<T>` methods.

```csharp
[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public OrdersController(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    [HttpGet("{id:int}")]
    public async Task<ActionResult<Order>> Get(int id)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
        return order is null ? NotFound() : Ok(order);
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public async Task<ActionResult<Order>> Create(CreateOrderDto dto)
    {
        var order = new Order { CustomerName = dto.CustomerName, Total = dto.Total };
        _db.Orders.Add(order);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
        return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = order.Id }, order);
    }

    [HttpPut("{id:int}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Update(int id, UpdateOrderDto dto)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
        if (order is null) return NotFound();

        order.CustomerName = dto.CustomerName;
        order.Total = dto.Total;
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
        return NoContent();
    }

    [HttpDelete("{id:int}")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Delete(int id)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
        if (order is null) return NotFound();

        _db.Orders.Remove(order);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
        return NoContent();
    }
}
```

Key behaviors worth internalizing:

- `FindAsync` looks up an entity **by primary key**, first checking the context's local change-tracking cache before issuing a query, which can save a round trip if the entity was already loaded earlier in the same request.
- Modifying properties on an entity retrieved via `FindAsync`/a tracked query does not hit the database immediately -- EF Core's change tracker records the modification, and only `SaveChangesAsync()` actually issues the `UPDATE` statement, wrapped in an implicit transaction if multiple changes are involved.
- `Add` followed by `SaveChangesAsync` issues an `INSERT`, and EF Core automatically populates any database-generated key (an identity column, for instance) back onto the entity instance afterward -- which is exactly why `order.Id` is already correctly populated by the time `CreatedAtAction` builds the response's `Location` header.
- `Remove` followed by `SaveChangesAsync` issues a `DELETE` for the tracked entity's current key value.

This pattern works well for simple APIs, but as logic grows -- validation, business rules, side effects like sending notifications -- many teams introduce a service layer between the controller and the `DbContext` so the controller stays a thin HTTP-to-domain translation layer rather than accumulating business logic directly.

codeExamples.code:
[HttpPost]
public async Task<ActionResult<Order>> Create(CreateOrderDto dto)
{
    var order = new Order { CustomerName = dto.CustomerName, Total = dto.Total };
    _db.Orders.Add(order);
    await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Get), new { id = order.Id }, order);
}

[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
public async Task<ActionResult<Order>> Get(int id)
{
    var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
    return order is null ? NotFound() : Ok(order);
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
ASP.NET Core (via Kestrel) handles concurrent requests using a thread pool: each incoming request is processed by a thread pool thread for as long as it is doing CPU work, but when a request awaits a genuinely asynchronous I/O operation (a database query, an HTTP call to another service, reading a file), the thread is released back to the pool to handle other requests while the I/O completes in the background, and a thread (not necessarily the same one) resumes the request once the awaited operation finishes.

Calling a **synchronous** EF Core method (`ToList()`, `SaveChanges()`, `Find()`) inside an action blocks the calling thread for the entire duration of the database round trip -- that thread sits idle, unable to serve any other request, purely waiting on network I/O to the database server. Under light load this is invisible; under concurrent load, it directly reduces the number of simultaneous requests the server can actually process, because thread pool threads are a finite, relatively expensive resource, and every blocked thread is one fewer available to handle other incoming requests. In extreme cases, this manifests as thread pool starvation: incoming requests queue up waiting for a free thread pool thread, and latency climbs sharply even though the database itself is not actually overloaded.

```csharp
// Synchronous: blocks a thread pool thread for the full DB round trip
[HttpGet]
public ActionResult<IEnumerable<Order>> GetAll() => Ok(_db.Orders.ToList());

// Asynchronous: releases the thread pool thread while awaiting I/O,
// allowing it to serve other requests in the meantime
[HttpGet]
public async Task<ActionResult<IEnumerable<Order>>> GetAll() => Ok(await _db.Orders.ToListAsync());
```

The fix is mechanical and low-risk: use the `Async` suffix variants EF Core provides for essentially every I/O-bound operation (`ToListAsync`, `FirstOrDefaultAsync`, `FindAsync` -- note `FindAsync` is already the async form, there is no synchronous "Find" distinction issue here beyond naming -- `SingleOrDefaultAsync`, `SaveChangesAsync`, `CountAsync`, `AnyAsync`), mark the action method `async`, and `await` each call. This is a case where there is essentially no downside to defaulting to async throughout a Web API's data-access code, since the framework, EF Core, and ASP.NET Core's hosting model are all designed around this asynchronous-by-default assumption, and the cost of forgetting it only shows up as a performance cliff under concurrent production load, not as a functional bug visible in local testing with a single user.

When you load an entity through a tracked query (anything not using `.AsNoTracking()`), EF Core's change tracker takes a **snapshot** of that entity's property values at the moment it is materialized from the database result set. This snapshot is stored internally alongside the entity in the context's identity map, separate from the entity instance's current, live property values.

When `SaveChangesAsync()` is called, the change tracker compares each tracked entity's **current** property values against its stored **original** snapshot, property by property. Only properties whose current value differs from the snapshot are included in the generated `UPDATE` statement's `SET` clause -- untouched properties are omitted entirely from the SQL, not merely left unchanged by happenstance.

```csharp
var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(5);
// Snapshot taken here: { Id = 5, CustomerName = "Priya", Total = 100.00m, Status = "Pending" }

order.Total = 150.00m;
// Current in-memory state: { Id = 5, CustomerName = "Priya", Total = 150.00m, Status = "Pending" }

await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
// EF Core diffs current vs. snapshot and generates:
// UPDATE Orders SET Total = 150.00 WHERE Id = 5;
// -- note CustomerName and Status are NOT included in the SET clause at all,
// -- because their current values match the original snapshot exactly.
```

This selective-update behavior is precisely why EF Core does not simply "overwrite every column" -- doing so would be both wasteful (sending unchanged data over the wire and rewriting unchanged index/storage pages) and actively dangerous in concurrent scenarios: if two different requests load the same row, each modifies a *different* unrelated column, and both subsequently save, a naive full-row overwrite would cause the second save to silently clobber the first request's change with the stale value it happened to read earlier. Column-level diffing avoids this specific class of lost-update bug by construction, as long as each request only reads and writes the columns it actually cares about.

The change tracker's snapshot comparison is also the mechanism underlying EF Core's **optimistic concurrency** support: if an entity has a property mapped with `[Timestamp]`/`[ConcurrencyCheck]` (or configured via the fluent API as a concurrency token), the generated `UPDATE` statement's `WHERE` clause additionally checks that the concurrency token column still matches the originally-read value, and if another transaction modified the row in between (changing the token), zero rows match the `WHERE` clause, which EF Core detects and surfaces as a `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` rather than silently succeeding against stale data.

```csharp
public class Order
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    [Timestamp]
    public byte[] RowVersion { get; set; } = default!;
}
// Generated UPDATE includes: WHERE Id = @id AND RowVersion = @originalRowVersion
// If RowVersion no longer matches (someone else updated the row first),
// zero rows are affected, and EF Core throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException.
```

Understanding this snapshot-diffing model explains both why `.AsNoTracking()` queries cannot be directly modified and saved (there is no snapshot to diff against, since the context never tracked the entity in the first place) and why re-attaching a detached entity with `_db.Update(entity)` marks **every** property as modified by default -- because without an original snapshot to compare against, EF Core has no way to know which properties actually changed, so it conservatively assumes all of them did.

lessons.contentMarkdown:
The repository pattern introduces an interface between your application's business logic and your data access technology, so that code consuming the repository depends only on an abstraction (`IOrderRepository`) rather than directly on `DbContext` or EF Core types.

```csharp
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id);
    Task<IReadOnlyList<Order>> GetAllAsync();
    Task AddAsync(Order order);
    Task<bool> DeleteAsync(int id);
}

public class EfOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public EfOrderRepository(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    public Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id) => _db.Orders.FindAsync(id).AsTask();

    public async Task<IReadOnlyList<Order>> GetAllAsync() => await _db.Orders.ToListAsync();

    public async Task AddAsync(Order order)
    {
        _db.Orders.Add(order);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
    }

    public async Task<bool> DeleteAsync(int id)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
        if (order is null) return false;
        _db.Orders.Remove(order);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
        return true;
    }
}
```

Registration and consumption follow ordinary DI conventions:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderRepository, EfOrderRepository>();

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IOrderRepository _repository;
    public OrdersController(IOrderRepository repository) => _repository = repository;

    [HttpGet("{id:int}")]
    public async Task<ActionResult<Order>> Get(int id)
    {
        var order = await _repository.GetByIdAsync(id);
        return order is null ? NotFound() : Ok(order);
    }
}
```

The commonly cited benefits are: controllers and services become easier to unit test, since `IOrderRepository` can be mocked without standing up an actual database or an in-memory EF Core provider; the data-access technology is, in theory, swappable without touching calling code; and query logic specific to a given entity type is centralized in one place rather than scattered across every controller that touches `Orders`.

In practice, the depth of this benefit depends heavily on how the repository interface is designed -- a repository whose methods just mirror `DbSet<T>` one-to-one (`GetAll`, `GetById`, `Add`, `Delete`) adds an abstraction layer without adding much real flexibility, since it is effectively a thin wrapper that still assumes EF Core-like semantics underneath. A more valuable repository exposes methods that reflect actual business queries (`GetOverdueOrdersAsync()`, `GetOrdersForCustomerAsync(customerId)`) rather than generic CRUD, which is a design decision worth making deliberately rather than defaulting to.

codeExamples.code:
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id);
    Task AddAsync(Order order);
}

public class EfOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public EfOrderRepository(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;
    public Task<Order?> GetByIdAsync(int id) => _db.Orders.FindAsync(id).AsTask();
    public async Task AddAsync(Order order) { _db.Orders.Add(order); await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); }
}

// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderRepository, EfOrderRepository>();

public class OrderService
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public OrderService(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    public Task<List<OrderSummaryDto>> GetRecentAsync(int customerId) =>
        _db.Orders.Where(o => o.CustomerId == customerId)
            .OrderByDescending(o => o.CreatedAt)
            .Select(o => new OrderSummaryDto(o.Id, o.Total))
            .AsNoTracking()
            .Take(20)
            .ToListAsync();
}

interviewQuestions.answer:
The Repository pattern describes an abstraction that mediates between application logic and a data source, exposing something like "get an entity by id," "add an entity," and "query entities matching some criteria," while hiding the underlying storage/query mechanics from the caller. The Unit of Work pattern describes a single, cohesive set of operations that are tracked together and committed (or rolled back) as one atomic group.

A `DbContext`'s `DbSet<T>` properties already provide exactly the repository-shaped operations -- `_db.Orders.Find(id)`, `_db.Orders.Add(order)`, `_db.Orders.Where(o => ...)` -- against an underlying data source, while hiding the actual SQL generation and connection management from the caller. And the `DbContext` itself already functions as a unit of work: every change made across however many different `DbSet<T>` properties you touch during a single context's lifetime is tracked together, and a single call to `SaveChangesAsync()` commits all of them together, typically within one database transaction.

```csharp
// This single DbContext usage already exhibits both patterns:
// - Repository-like access via DbSet<T> (Orders, Customers)
// - Unit of Work via one SaveChangesAsync() committing both changes together
var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(orderId);
order.Status = OrderStatus.Shipped;

var customer = await _db.Customers.FindAsync(order.CustomerId);
customer.LastOrderShippedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;

await _db.SaveChangesAsync(); // both changes commit together, or neither does
```

Why this matters for the repository-layer decision: introducing a custom `IOrderRepository`/`ICustomerRepository` pair that simply forwards one-to-one to `DbSet<Order>`/`DbSet<Customer>` calls does not add a new capability that was missing before -- it adds a second layer of indirection around a capability `DbContext` already provides, with real costs (more code to maintain, and often losing easy access to EF Core-specific features like `Include()` or `AsNoTracking()` unless the repository interface is deliberately widened to support them). This is why many experienced .NET developers specifically push back on reflexively adding a generic repository layer "because it's a best practice," and instead ask what concrete capability a given project's custom repository would add beyond what `DbContext` already provides on its own.

**Against a generic `IRepository<T>`:** `DbContext`/`DbSet<T>` already implements the Repository and Unit of Work patterns. A generic wrapper (`IRepository<T>` with `GetAll()`, `GetById()`, `Add()`, `Update()`, `Delete()`) typically cannot expose EF Core's genuinely useful capabilities -- eager loading via `Include()`, `AsNoTracking()` for read performance, `Select` projections directly into DTOs, bulk `ExecuteUpdateAsync`/`ExecuteDeleteAsync`, raw SQL escape hatches -- without either becoming a leaky, EF Core-shaped interface in disguise (which defeats the stated goal of hiding EF Core) or forcing developers to bypass the repository entirely whenever they need those features, which in practice happens often enough that the abstraction provides a false sense of decoupling while adding a mandatory extra layer of indirection and boilerplate for every entity type.

```csharp
// A generic repository that cannot express a common real need:
// "give me order summaries only, with no tracking, for a specific customer"
public interface IRepository<T> where T : class
{
    Task<T?> GetByIdAsync(int id);
    Task<List<T>> GetAllAsync(); // no way to project, filter, or disable tracking here
}
```

**For some repository abstraction:** even granting that `DbContext` is itself a repository, there is real value in a narrower, purpose-built abstraction around a specific, cohesive aggregate when you need one of: (a) a seam for genuinely swapping or layering an implementation, such as wrapping reads in a caching decorator (`CachingOrderRepository` wrapping `EfOrderRepository`) without touching every caller; (b) encapsulating a complex aggregate's invariants so that "saving an Order" always correctly cascades its OrderLines and recalculates a derived Total, rather than trusting every caller across the codebase to remember that logic; or (c) isolating a bounded context's persistence so that domain logic in that context has zero compile-time dependency on EF Core, which matters more in architectures that deliberately isolate the domain layer (e.g., Clean Architecture/DDD-styled codebases).

**Reconciling the two in practice:** most experienced teams land on a hybrid: use `DbContext` directly, via a thin service layer (not controllers), for ordinary queries and simple CRUD, where EF Core's own abstraction is already sufficient -- and introduce a dedicated, purpose-specific abstraction (not a generic `IRepository<T>`) only around the handful of aggregates where business rules, caching, or genuine technology-swapping concerns justify the extra indirection. This avoids paying the cost of an abstraction layer everywhere while still getting its benefits exactly where they matter.

A repository earns its keep when its interface reflects the aggregate's actual business operations and invariants, rather than mirroring generic CRUD. For an `Order` aggregate that must always persist its `OrderLines` together with a correctly recalculated `Total`, and where a specific business process ("mark as shipped") has its own rules, a well-designed repository looks like this:

```csharp
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetWithLinesAsync(int orderId);
    Task<IReadOnlyList<Order>> GetOverdueOrdersAsync(DateTime asOf);
    Task AddAsync(Order order);
    Task MarkAsShippedAsync(int orderId, DateTime shippedAt);
}

public class EfOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;
    public EfOrderRepository(AppDbContext db) => _db = db;

    // Encapsulates the correct Include() shape for this aggregate --
    // callers never need to know OrderLines must be eagerly loaded here.
    public Task<Order?> GetWithLinesAsync(int orderId) =>
        _db.Orders.Include(o => o.Lines).FirstOrDefaultAsync(o => o.Id == orderId);

    // A genuine business query, not a generic filter -- the "overdue" definition
    // lives in exactly one place instead of being reimplemented by every caller.
    public Task<IReadOnlyList<Order>> GetOverdueOrdersAsync(DateTime asOf) =>
        _db.Orders
            .Where(o => o.Status == OrderStatus.Pending && o.DueDate < asOf)
            .ToListAsync()
            .ContinueWith(t => (IReadOnlyList<Order>)t.Result);

    public async Task AddAsync(Order order)
    {
        order.RecalculateTotal(); // domain invariant enforced before persistence
        _db.Orders.Add(order);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
    }

    // Encapsulates the specific, auditable state transition rather than
    // letting any caller set Status directly to whatever value it wants.
    public async Task MarkAsShippedAsync(int orderId, DateTime shippedAt)
    {
        var order = await _db.Orders.FindAsync(orderId)
            ?? throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Order {orderId} not found.");
        order.MarkAsShipped(shippedAt); // domain method enforces valid state transitions
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

What makes this genuinely valuable, as opposed to a thin pass-through wrapper:

1. **`GetWithLinesAsync` encapsulates a loading concern** (which navigation properties must be eagerly included for this aggregate to be usable) in one place, so every caller gets a correctly-loaded `Order` without needing to know or remember EF Core's `Include()` requirements.
2. **`GetOverdueOrdersAsync` expresses a business concept directly**, meaning the definition of "overdue" lives in exactly one method, not duplicated as a `Where` clause scattered across several controllers or services that would otherwise each need to reimplement it identically.
3. **`MarkAsShippedAsync` protects an invariant**: shipping an order is a specific state transition with its own rules (perhaps it cannot ship an order that has not been paid), enforced by a domain method (`order.MarkAsShipped(shippedAt)`) that the repository calls, rather than trusting every caller to set `order.Status = OrderStatus.Shipped` correctly and remember every side effect that should accompany it.

This is the kind of repository worth writing deliberately, per aggregate, as opposed to a generic `IRepository<T>` applied uniformly across every entity in the system regardless of whether that entity has any real business invariants worth protecting.

